== quirks of this loop [DRILL]

Team variability is the recorded norm, not a caveat: the FAQ says
each hiring manager shapes the loop, expect variability in
emphasis and question style between teams, so two Apple screens
can be different interviews with nothing shared but the badge. The
FAQ's subtopic list is the hardware edge no other arc page
carries, memory systems and microarchitecture topics like
out-of-order execution and cache behavior, embedded and firmware
debugging, SystemVerilog verification basics, and device-oriented
design such as sensor subsystems and thermal and signal tradeoffs.
The corpus floor for the memory half is #xref-to("repertoire",
"systems-answers"), and the gap is named here: the SystemVerilog
and firmware-debugging half has no home in this book.

The visible experiences are the quirks in the first person. A
Java-only CoderPad screen ran a progressive store-hours problem.
A straight-to-code phone screen ran a multi-family unit
conversion graph in Java and is still in progress. A single
hiring manager round on Terraform provisioning ended in a
rejection. An intern fumbled C++ trivia and still got great
feedback, the loop grading recovery over recall. A resume deep
dive handed over a plant-poisoning grid problem. A bug-free LRU
was still a rejection, the plainest proof that correctness clears
a bar, not the bar. A Data Scientist screen explored employee and
audit tables with the hiring manager. The one virtual onsite
paired a top-k urls design with an html parser and an LRU cache,
the same three shapes the card list sells as practice.

