== worked prompt: unit ratio queries across independent families [TDD]

The visible card, from the page 1 card table: "Unit Ratio Queries
Across Independent Unit Families with Modular Answers", medium,
Coding and Algorithms plus Graphs plus Math and Number Theory, 7
likes, 105 solvers, Sep 27, 2026. The visible body confirms the
setup and stops mid sentence at "The units fall into several
independent families: units in the same family can be converted
into on...", so the contract below is authored from the title plus
that fragment, and its decisions are stated as authored. The
straight-to-code phone screen experience, a multi-family unit
conversion graph in Java, is this problem family reported live.

The contract: a conversion is an edge with a rational weight,
1 ft = 12 in carried as numerator and denominator, and families are
connected components, nothing else. A query asks how many of the
second unit one of the first converts to, folded modulo a given
modulus at the answer, and a query across families is an error,
never a guess. Insertion validates what the title asserts:
positive ratios only, no self conversions, and an edge that would
tie already-connected units to a different ratio is rejected as a
conflict, because path consistency is what lets any walk through
the family answer the query:

#listing("interview-repertoire/samples/ch49-go/units.go", first: 32, last: 49, caption: [insertion: validate, reduce by the gcd, reject a conflicting ratio, append both directions])

The ratio itself is a breadth-first walk that accumulates exact
rationals, big.Rat, so no product overflows on the way and the
modular fold happens once, at the answer, which is the title's
"modular answers" honored literally:

#listing("interview-repertoire/samples/ch49-go/units.go", first: 78, last: 99, caption: [the exact walk: one rational per unit, any path carries the same ratio])

The fold reduces numerator and denominator mod m, inverts the
denominator, and multiplies, the number-theory spine of
#xref-to("dsa", "numtheory"). The inverse exists only when the
denominator and modulus share no factor, and a modulus that breaks
that is the caller's error to see, not a wrong answer to receive.
Mod 1 returns 0 by the everything-is-zero convention, and a self
query is 1 mod m:

#listing("interview-repertoire/samples/ch49-go/units.go", first: 51, last: 76, caption: [the query: guards, the exact ratio, then numerator times the modular inverse of the denominator])

The cost is one breadth-first walk per query over the family it
lands in, O(V+E) with rational terms that grow with path length,
and the follow-up worth volunteering is the competitive cut: fold
each edge's factor mod m as the walk goes, with 128-bit
intermediate products, and the terms never grow at all, correct
because congruence composes over multiplication. The graph
traversal underneath is #xref-to("repertoire", "graphs").

The suite, 11 test functions under vet and the race detector,
builds every expected value by hand, the oracle discipline this
book always demands: the length and time families with three-hop
ratios like 63360 and 86400, modular reductions at 1000 and 7, an
inverse table derived on paper, 12x = 1 mod 1e9+7 at x = 83333334
because 12 times it is exactly 1e9+8, the family-merge case where
a bridge edge joins two families at 3 * 7 * 5, the conflict and
validation tables, and the not-invertible cases where the modulus
shares a factor with the denominator. Red first: the suite failed
11 of 11 functions against a compiling stub, and the green run
earned one correction in this chapter's own arithmetic, a
reverse-direction expectation first written as the forward value,
caught failing, and re-derived from the modular identity it must
satisfy, 105 times 209523811 is 1 mod 1e9+7, the direction-of-fit
discipline doing its job.

== worked prompt: first success under one global timeout [TDD]

The visible card: "Return the First Successful Result from Parallel
API Calls Under a Global Timeout", medium, Software Engineering
Fundamentals plus Concurrency, 4 likes, 70 solvers, Sep 13, 2026.
The visible body sets the scene and stops mid sentence at "To get
an answer quickly and reliably, it makes seve...", and the
authored contract reads the title's three load-bearing words,
parallel, first successful, and global.

The contract: every call runs at once, the winner is the first
non-error result, and the deadline is one clock for the whole
race, not one per call. The distinction is spend, not semantics:
three sequential attempts with a 30 ms budget each can spend 90 ms,
one global 30 ms deadline spends 30 ms and hands back the timeout.
Every call is a function of its context, failures are collected
and joined into the all-failed error, and the stragglers see the
cancellation the moment a winner exists:

#listing("interview-repertoire/samples/ch49-go/parallel.go", first: 23, last: 49, caption: [fan out once, one buffered slot per call, first non-error wins, the deferred cancel drains the stragglers])

#callout("pitfall", "the two ways this prompt is failed", [
  The first is the unbuffered channel: a loser that sends after
  the winner returned blocks forever, a goroutine leak the race
  detector never shows because nothing races, and the buffer of
  len(calls) is the whole fix. The second is the per-call timeout:
  restarting the clock per attempt quietly triples the budget,
  the title says one global timeout, one clock, started once,
  spent once.
])

The cancellation reaches the calls through the context they
receive, and the suite pins the leak contract directly: a
straggler that blocks on ctx.Done() must observe the cancellation
after the winner returns, and the test waits on the straggler's
own done channel to prove the goroutine ended. The suite is 7
test functions under vet and the race detector: the ordered
winner with scripted delays, survival past an immediate failure,
the joined all-failed error carrying every sentinel, the timeout
beating a slow success, the no-calls error, the straggler
cancellation, and a concurrent hammer of 8 callers over one
shared call set. Red first: 7 of 7 functions failed against the
compiling stub. The concurrency vocabulary is
#xref-to("go", "concurrency") and the pattern catalog placement
is #xref-to("patterns", "concpatterns").

== design: the centralized logging system [DRILL]

The prompt, the page's most-liked design card by a wide margin:
"Design a Centralized Logging System", medium, locked, System
Design plus Observability plus Messaging and Event-Driven, 47
likes and 556 solvers by the card table. The lock means the body
is premium gated, so the requirements below are authored from the
title, and the protocol is the one from #xref-to("repertoire",
"design-prompts"): requirements, arithmetic before boxes, the
topology, the walk, the bottleneck volunteered, traps one line
each.

Requirements, functional plus the stated assumptions, mine, not
the page's: collect logs from every app instance, 50,000 of them
at an average 10 lines per second each, ship within seconds,
search the last 7 days in seconds, keep 90 days cold, and never
let logging hurt the application it logs for.

The arithmetic, the method of #xref-to("repertoire",
"estimation-answers"). 50,000 instances at 10 lines per second is
500,000 lines per second peak. At 200 bytes a line that is 100 MB
per second sustained, 8.6 TB a day, about 260 TB a month, so the
90-day cold tier lands near 780 TB and only compression and object
storage make it sane, json logs compress roughly 10 to 1. The hot
7 days at 8.6 TB a day is 60 TB in a columnar store partitioned by
hour and service, and one hour of one service is the unit every
query touches: 500,000 lines per second over 2,000 services is 250
lines per second per service, 900,000 lines an hour, about 180 MB
raw, a partition any node holds in memory.

#flow(
  [logs out: never block the app, a durable queue in the middle, the hot store partitioned by hour and service],
  node((0, 0), [app, #linebreak() bounded buffer,#linebreak() drop-oldest]),
  node((0, 1.2), [agent, #linebreak() batch, compress,#linebreak() disk spill]),
  node((0, 2.4), [queue, #linebreak() partitioned by service,#linebreak() days of retention]),
  node((0, 3.6), [indexer pool, #linebreak() parse, index,#linebreak() aggregate]),
  node((0, 4.8), [hot store, #linebreak() 7 days, hourly,#linebreak() per-service partitions]),
  node((2.6, 4.8), [object storage, #linebreak() 90 days, compressed]),
  node((0, 6.0), [query api, #linebreak() scatter-gather,#linebreak() per-partition timeout]),
  edge((0, 0), (0, 1.2), "->"),
  edge((0, 1.2), (0, 2.4), "->"),
  edge((0, 2.4), (0, 3.6), "->"),
  edge((0, 3.6), (0, 4.8), "->"),
  edge((0, 4.8), (2.6, 4.8), "->", label: [after 7 days]),
  edge((0, 6.0), (0, 4.8), "->"),
)

The walk, hop by hop. The app writes into a bounded in-process
buffer and never blocks, when the buffer is full the oldest line
drops and a counter ticks, because the alternative is the logging
library timing the application. The agent batches, compresses, and
holds a disk spill so a dead queue means local accumulation, not
lost lines. The queue is the durability boundary, partitioned by
service so one chatty producer cannot starve the rest, with days
of retention to ride out an indexer outage. The indexers parse,
write the hourly per-service partitions, and emit the aggregates
dashboards read, so dashboards never scan raw lines. The query api
scatter-gathers across the partitions a time range selects, with a
per-partition timeout so one slow shard answers empty instead of
holding the search. The metrics and alerting lane taps the same
queue, the shapes are #xref-to("patterns", "observability") and
the running lane this corpus builds is #xref-to("go", "obs").

The bottleneck, volunteered: partition skew at the hot store's
write path. Time-plus-service partitioning lands a retry storm or
one newly chatty release onto a handful of partitions, and the fix
is shard keys under the partition, sub-partition by instance
hash, plus admission shaping at the agent when one service's rate
steps an order of magnitude. The read side's wall is the fan-out,
a 24-hour search over 2,000 services is 48,000 partition probes,
which is why the api takes a service set first and a time range
second.

Traps, one line each. Blocking the app for a log line is the
original sin, the bounded buffer and the drop counter are the
whole religion. Global ordering is a lie across 50,000 clocks,
per-host sequence numbers give local order, say so. Sampling
before shipping saves the budget but lies in counts, sampled
lines carry weights or they answer "how many" wrongly. Clock skew
breaks cross-host correlation, tolerate second-level windows or
carry a logical timestamp. Schema fights are avoided by fixed
envelope fields plus a json blob, schema-on-read for the blob.

== design: the hotel room booking system [DRILL]

The prompt: "Object-Oriented Design: Hotel Room Booking System",
medium, locked, System Design plus Low-Level Design (OOP) plus
Concurrency, 19 likes and 243 solvers by the card table. The
low-level tag names the round, this is an object model with an
invariant, not a fleet, and the honest arithmetic is one line said
aloud: a 200-room hotel over a year-long horizon is 200 times 365,
73,000 room-nights, the whole domain fits in memory, which is the
design's first finding, the difficulty is correctness under
concurrency, not scale.

Requirements, authored: rooms with a type and a rate, search by
type and date range, book through a hold that expires, confirm,
cancel under a policy, checkout. The model, floored on
#xref-to("repertoire", "paradigms"): Hotel owns Room instances,
Room has identity, type, and floor but never a calendar, the
calendar is a query over Booking records, Booking owns the guest,
the room, the nights as hotel-local dates, and its lifecycle
states, and a RateTable maps type and season to price. Storing
nights as dates, not instants, is the timezone answer before the
traps section earns it.

The invariant is the system: a room-night sells at most once.
Availability is interval overlap per room, one booking's night
set against another's, the arithmetic #xref-to("repertoire",
"algopatterns") owns, and every mutation that grants a hold
re-checks it under one per-hotel serialization point.

#flow(
  [the booking lifecycle: a 15-minute hold is the concurrency answer],
  node((0, 0), [search, #linebreak() type, date range,#linebreak() overlap per room]),
  node((0, 1.2), [hold, #linebreak() 15 min lease,#linebreak() room-nights reserved]),
  node((0, 2.4), [confirm, #linebreak() idempotent by id,#linebreak() re-check the lease]),
  node((0, 3.6), [occupy, #linebreak() checkout]),
  node((2.6, 1.2), [cancel, #linebreak() free inside hold,#linebreak() priced after]),
  node((2.6, 2.4), [expiry, #linebreak() lease lapse on read,#linebreak() plus a sweep]),
  edge((0, 0), (0, 1.2), "->"),
  edge((0, 1.2), (0, 2.4), "->"),
  edge((0, 2.4), (0, 3.6), "->"),
  edge((2.6, 1.2), (0, 1.2), "-|>"),
  edge((0, 1.2), (2.6, 2.4), "->", label: [no confirm]),
)

The walk. Search computes overlap per room of the requested type
and returns the free ones, read-only, no state. Hold is the write:
under the per-hotel lock it re-runs the overlap check and grants a
15-minute lease on the room-nights, and the lease is how two guests
racing for the last room both get an answer, one holds it, one
gets a clean no. Confirm re-checks the lease under the same lock
and mints the booking idempotently, a retried confirm returns the
same booking, never a second one. Expiry is lazy, a lapsed hold is
detected on read and swept periodically, no cleanup thread holds
authority over the invariant.

The bottleneck, volunteered: at interview scale there is none, and
saying so is the answer, the serialization point is per hotel and
one hotel's booking rate is human-sized. At 10,000 hotels the same
invariant shards per hotel with zero cross-hotel coordination, and
the fleet question the follow-up asks is answered by not needing
to ask it.

Traps, one line each. Double booking is the trap the round exists
for, the hold plus one serialized grant point is the answer.
Timezones and midnight, nights are hotel-local dates, never utc
instants, a checkout at 1 am is still the same night. Overbooking
by a few percent is a product decision, an intentional policy, not
a bug to fix on the whiteboard. Payment ordering, confirm
re-checks the lease under the lock, the payment service sits
outside it and a failed payment must lapse the hold cleanly.
Cancellation policy is a state machine on the booking, free inside
the hold, priced after, and the rate belongs to type and season,
never to the individual room.

